## 1. Big picture

### 1.1. What is unsupervised learning?

**Supervised learning** uses (input, label) pairs and tries to learn a mapping from input to label (e.g. classify cats vs. dogs). **Unsupervised learning** uses only inputs — no labels — and tries to find structure on its own.

**Clustering** is the most common unsupervised task: group similar items together without being told what the groups are.

### 1.2. Our concrete task

We have a folder of images organised by class. We pretend we do not know the class labels and try to recover them with clustering. At the very end, we use the real labels only to *check* how well our clustering recovered the true classes — this is called **external validation**.

### 1.3. The full pipeline

```
raw images
   │
   ▼
[EDA]                         understand the dataset, check quality
   │
   ▼
[ResNet50]                    turn each image into a 2048-D feature vector
   │
   ▼
[StandardScaler → PCA]        normalise and reduce to 50 dimensions
   │
   ├──▶ [K-Means]              partition into k spherical clusters
   ├──▶ [Hierarchical]         build a tree, cut it at k clusters
   └──▶ [DBSCAN]               find dense regions, mark sparse points as noise
   │
   ▼
[Internal + External metrics] compare the three methods
   │
   ▼
[Final choice]               K-Means with k = 30
```

### 1.4. Why this pipeline?

- Raw images live in pixel space, which has thousands of dimensions per image and no semantic structure (pixel `[100, 200]` of two cat photos can be very different).
- A pre-trained CNN like ResNet50 has already learned to map images into a feature space where **similar things are close together**. So clustering becomes possible.
- PCA reduces dimensionality, which makes clustering faster and helps fight the "curse of dimensionality".
- Using three different clustering algorithms is good practice: each one makes different assumptions, so if they agree we know the structure is real.

