## 11. Likely exam / defence questions

Here are the questions I would prepare for. Each comes with a short model answer that you can expand on.

### Q1. Why did you use ResNet50 instead of training your own CNN?

Training a CNN from scratch on ~25,000 images is not enough data — it would overfit. ResNet50 pre-trained on ImageNet has already learned generic visual features (edges, textures, shapes) from 1.2 million images. By using it as a fixed feature extractor we transfer that knowledge to our problem without needing to train anything ourselves. This is called transfer learning.

### Q2. Why did you remove the classifier head?

The final layer of ResNet50 outputs 1000 logits corresponding to ImageNet classes (cat, dog, traffic light…). We do not care about those classes. We want the **features** that come just before the classifier — those features represent visual content in general.

### Q3. Why 224 × 224 images?

That is the input size ResNet50 was trained on. Using a different size would change the spatial dimensions of every feature map and the learned weights would no longer apply correctly.

### Q4. Why standardise before PCA?

PCA is sensitive to the scale of each feature: a feature with a large range will dominate the principal components even if it is not informative. Standardising makes all features have unit variance so PCA can find the directions of greatest *meaningful* variation.

### Q5. Why 50 PCA components specifically?

The scree plot shows that the first 50 components capture most of the variance, after which each additional component adds very little. 50 is also low enough to make distance-based clustering fast and reduce the curse of dimensionality. We don't claim 50 is optimal — it's a reasonable compromise that we justified visually.

### Q6. Why try three clustering methods?

Each method makes different assumptions:

- K-Means assumes spherical equal-sized clusters.
- Hierarchical Ward assumes variance-minimising compact clusters.
- DBSCAN assumes density-based clusters with arbitrary shape.

If different methods agree, we can be more confident the partition is real. If they disagree, the disagreement itself is informative (it tells us about the geometry of the feature space).

### Q7. How did you choose k for K-Means?

We did a coarse search over k ∈ {10, 20, 30, 40, 50} and picked the value with the highest silhouette score (k = 30). We then ran a finer search over k ∈ {5, 10, …, 50} with both an elbow plot and a silhouette curve to confirm that 30 sits in a sensible region of both. We also noted that 30 happens to match the number of true folder classes, which is reassuring but not the criterion we used.

### Q8. Why Ward linkage instead of Complete or Average?

Ward minimises within-cluster variance, which is the same objective K-Means optimises. So Ward and K-Means produce comparable cluster shapes. Empirically, Ward gave us the best Calinski-Harabasz score among the linkages, with competitive silhouette. Complete linkage scored worst across all metrics.

### Q9. Why did DBSCAN perform so badly?

DBSCAN is density-based and works best in low-dimensional spaces where distances are meaningful. In 50-D, distances between points concentrate (they all become similar), so the gap between dense and sparse regions shrinks. Combined with variable cluster density in the feature space, DBSCAN cannot find a single ε that works globally, and it labels most points as noise. This is a structural mismatch between the algorithm and the data, not a failure of hyperparameter tuning.

### Q10. Why compute metrics on non-noise points for DBSCAN?

Noise points (label `-1`) are not a real cluster — they are explicitly "I don't know". Including them in the silhouette computation would mix in many points that DBSCAN deliberately refused to cluster, which would distort the score. So we restrict to non-noise points. We also report the noise fraction separately so we don't hide it.

### Q11. The dataset has labels — why didn't you use them in training?

Because the project is about *unsupervised* learning. The whole point is to find structure without seeing the labels. We use the labels only at the very end, for **external validation** with ARI and NMI, to check whether our unsupervised method discovered the same structure that the human-assigned labels represent.

### Q12. What is the difference between Silhouette and ARI?

- **Silhouette** is an **internal** metric: it uses only the data and the cluster assignments. It measures geometric quality (compact, well-separated).
- **ARI** is an **external** metric: it requires a ground-truth labelling and compares two partitions.

You can have a high silhouette but low ARI (you found a geometric structure that does not correspond to the human classes) or low silhouette but high ARI (the classes are real but the geometry is hard).

### Q13. Why did you set a random seed?

K-Means uses random initialisation of the centroids; t-SNE uses random initial positions; some of our subsampling uses random choice. Without a fixed seed, every re-run produces slightly different numbers, which makes it impossible to reproduce results. `RANDOM_STATE = 42` is set globally and propagated to every random-number generator.

### Q14. What's the point of the cluster image grid?

Internal metrics measure geometric quality but not semantic quality. The image grid lets us see whether a "good" cluster according to silhouette really contains visually similar images of (mostly) one true class. It bridges the gap between numerical metrics and human judgement.

### Q15. If you had more time, what would you do differently?

Honest answers (course-appropriate):

- Try a more modern feature extractor (DINOv2, CLIP) which produces feature spaces better aligned with semantic similarity.
- Replace DBSCAN with HDBSCAN, which handles variable density.
- Use UMAP instead of PCA before clustering — it often preserves cluster structure better.
- Run K-Means multiple times with different seeds and report stability statistics (mean ± std), not just a single point estimate.

These are extensions we recognise but did not implement because the course brief specified ResNet50 + K-Means / Hierarchical / DBSCAN.

